## Framing challenge

The brief assumes the missing step is a better remedy. The evidence points elsewhere: pass 1's eight findings were all "fixed", and pass 2 found the *remaining cases*. The defect is verification of the fix, not imagination of it. Options the brief does not list:

- Finder ships acceptance criteria + invariant + test skeleton with the finding (no new consultation).
- One solver, not a panel of three; or the worker proposes-then-codes in the same wave.
- Remedy panel triggered only on a *reopened* finding (F91-1/F91-2 qualify), not every blocker.
- Do nothing: spend the same window on a longer finder verification pass.

To choose I would need: the re-failure rate per finding class, solver proposal adoption rate in a one-release pilot, pack-only vs repo-reading solver quality, and each lab's real window scarcity. Without those, build the pilot, not the machinery.

## Q1. Roles and verifier

Separation is sound; vendor exclusion is the weak part (vendor is a poor proxy for independence, and may exclude the best solver — exclude the finder *instance* instead). Failure modes: pack-only solvers hallucinate code that is not there; finders reject foreign fixes that threaten their frame; three consultations per blocker at ~280 s and 9–16 % of a window each does not scale past a few blockers.

Verifier: dual-key. The solver's shipped test is primary evidence, the finder confirms its original trigger is closed against criteria it wrote *before* seeing proposals, and a third member adjudicates only on dispute or second failure. Test-only verification fails — this codebase's history is implementer tests encoding the implementer's frame.

## Q2. Minimal design

`proposals[]` per finding: `{finding_id, proposer{provider, model, consult_id}, base_commit, approach, locations[], invariant, patch_sketch, test{name, drives, asserts}, risks, confidence}`, stored verbatim in `findings.json`; coordinator adds `{status: adopted|rejected, reason}`. Brief = finding record + pack of located code and existing tests. Existing statuses (`proposed|implemented|verified`) carry the lifecycle.

Must NOT be automated: adoption, merging proposals, implementation, verification/closure, and the decision to spend the window. The `--remedy` offer on HOLD is the right level of automation.

## Q3. Live remedies

**F91-1 (blocker)** — Move attempt derivation under the lock: `claim_for_spawn` ([mod.rs](/crates/c3/src/work/mod.rs:747)) takes an expectation (resumable statuses, cap), rereads under `admission_lock`, refuses terminal/exhausted/foreign-live records, derives `attempt = locked quota.resumes + 1`, and writes `running` in the same locked write; `resume_once` ([mod.rs](/crates/c3/src/work/mod.rs:1137)) stops precomputing `n`. Invariant: no spawn unless the locked record was resumable at claim instant. Tests: `claim_for_spawn_rejects_terminal_and_exhausted`, `claim_for_spawn_derives_attempt_from_locked_record`.

**F91-2 (blocker)** — Fail closed on uninspectable records: `read_record` ([record.rs](/crates/c3/src/work/record.rs:137)) maps only `NotFound` to `Ok(None)`; `run_dirs`/`live_run`/`other_live_run` return `Result` and both admission paths refuse on error. Invariant: admission only over inspected-or-confirmed-absent records. Tests: `read_record_distinguishes_notfound_from_unreadable`, `admission_refuses_when_candidate_uninspectable`.

**F91-4 (major)** — Require affirmative terminal evidence: Claude success only from `subtype=="success"` with explicit `is_error:false` (new `has_success_result` in [record.rs](/crates/c3/src/work/record.rs:544)); Codex success only from `turn.completed`, deleting the `agent_message` arm in `terminal_outcome` ([engines.rs](/crates/c3/src/work/engines.rs:481)) while keeping `agent_message` for report extraction. Update `terminal_outcome_reads_the_stream` to expect `None` for both incomplete streams.

## Q4. Scoring

Split, delayed, denominated. Finder credit at verified closure of a real finding; solver credit only when an adopted proposal is implemented *and verified* without material redesign (`partly` if reworked). Adoption alone scores nothing. Weight by severity and recurrence; count rejected/duplicate findings and rejected proposals in the denominator so volume farming dilutes itself. Keep finder/solver/verifier as three router dimensions with a minimum sample before solver routing bites.

## Requested checks

- RC1: repo root; workspace-write; `cargo test -p c3 --lib work::` after implementing finding #4 and #6; expect the updated `terminal_outcome_reads_the_stream` plus claim/read-record tests green. Budget: 10 min. Settles finding #4 and #6 testability.
- RC2: repo root; workspace-write; barrier fixture pausing a second resume before `claim_for_spawn` while the first reaches `done`; expect refusal and no second launch marker. Budget: 10 min. Settles finding #4 (F91-1) end to end.

### Findings

- **F03-1** [note] `.collab/consilium-2026-10-22/handoffs/01-claude-remedy-proposals.md:58` - Gate the remedy panel on a repeat failure, not every blocker: alternatives the brief omits are (a) finder ships acceptance criteria + invariant + test skeleton with the finding, (b) one solver, not a panel of three, (c) the worker must propose-then-code in the same wave, and (d) do nothing beyond a better worker brief. Triggering only when a fix has already failed once (F91-1/F91-2 are exactly that) captures most value at a fraction of the 3x consultation cost. Trigger: Coordinator must choose between the proposed panel, a cheaper finder-brief improvement, and a single solver, with no data on which produces verified fixes. Evidence: read-code: Pass 1 had 3 blockers + 5 findings, the worker fixed all eight, pass 2 HOLD again with two 'remaining case' findings: the failure mode is a failed fix, not a missing first remedy. Verify: Pilot on the four F91 findings: record for each whether a solver proposal was adopted unmodified, partly, or rejected, and whether the implementing pass closed it. Compare against the pass-1-to-pass-2 re-failure baseline. Remedy: Add an automatic offer only on HOLD where a finding supersedes or reopens a prior one. Run a single-solver pilot for one release and measure adoption rate and re-failure rate before building panels, exclusion, or scoring.
- **F03-2** [note] (no location) - Q1: finder/solver separation is sound, but verification must be dual-key: the solver's shipped test is the primary evidence, the finder confirms the original trigger is closed, and a third member adjudicates only on dispute. Finder alone rejects foreign fixes and confirms its own frame; a solver-written test alone encodes the solver's wrong assumption as truth; a third verifier alone lacks trigger context. Trigger: A solver from another lab, possibly pack-only with no repo access, proposes a fix the finder did not frame; someone must decide it closes the original trigger. Evidence: inferred: The pass-2 findings are themselves 'the fix closed the simple case, this is the remaining case', i.e. implementer-side tests already encoded the implementer's frame once. Verify: On the pilot, log finder/solver-verifier disagreements: the rate at which the shipped test passes but the finder still reproduces the trigger measures test-only verification failure. Remedy: Finder states acceptance criteria (evidence that closes the finding) at raise time, before seeing proposals. Solver ships the named test. Finder verifies against its own criteria; disagreement or a second failure escalates to a third member. Solvers see claim and trigger but not the finder's one-line remedy, to avoid anchoring.
- **F03-3** [note] `.collab/consilium-2026-10-22/handoffs/01-claude-remedy-proposals.md:51` - Q2: the minimal proposals[] record is {finding_id, proposer{provider, model, consult_id}, base_commit, approach, locations[], invariant, patch_sketch, test{name, drives, asserts}, risks, confidence}; the coordinator adds {status: adopted|rejected, reason} and the existing finding status flow proposed|implemented|verified does the rest. No new ledger is needed. Trigger: A remedy consultation returns free text that the coordinator must store, choose from, and later credit, inside a files-are-the-record ledger. Evidence: read-code: Brief states findings[].remedy, prior_findings[] statuses, panel roles, routing, packs and findings.json already exist; only proposals[] storage and a remedy purpose are new. Verify: Round-trip one pilot consultation: proposals land in findings.json, coordinator marks adopted/rejected with reason, `c3 findings` shows the choice, and no step fires without an explicit coordinator command. Remedy: Store proposals verbatim under findings[].proposals[] keyed by finding id, generated brief = finding record + pack of located code and existing tests at base_commit. Must NOT be automated: adoption, merging, implementation, verification/closure, and the decision to spend the window. Vendor exclusion should prefer a different model instance, not hard-exclude a lab, since vendor is a weak proxy for independence and may exclude the best solver.
- **F03-4** [blocker] `crates/c3/src/work/mod.rs:747`, `crates/c3/src/work/mod.rs:1142`, `crates/c3/src/work/mod.rs:1386` - Q3/F91-1 remedy: move attempt derivation and resumability revalidation inside claim_for_spawn under the admission lock. claim_for_spawn (mod.rs:747) currently only rejects a different live owner and unconditionally sets running; resume_once (mod.rs:1142) computes n before the lock; do_resume_foreground (mod.rs:1386) checks status/exhaustion on a pre-lock record. Trigger: Two resumes pass pre-lock checks; the first finishes (done) or exhausts the cap while the second is paused before claim_for_spawn, which then resurrects it with a stale attempt number. Evidence: read-code: claim_for_spawn rereads the record under admission_lock but checks only other-live-run and live-owner before writing running with the caller-supplied attempt.; read-code: n = quota.resumes + 1 is computed at line 1142, before claim_for_spawn acquires the lock at line 1146; status and exhausted checks at 1386-1399 use the earlier record. Verify: New tests claim_for_spawn_rejects_terminal_and_exhausted (drive claim_for_spawn on done and on quota+exhausted records, assert Err and run.json unchanged) and claim_for_spawn_derives_attempt_from_locked_record (bump quota.resumes on disk after the caller's snapshot, assert the claimed attempt follows the locked value). Plus the barrier integration fixture from RC1 of handoff 91: delayed second resume refuses with no second launch marker. Remedy: Change claim_for_spawn to take the caller's expectation (resumable statuses, max_resumes) instead of a precomputed attempt: under the lock, reread, refuse if status is not resumable, if quota.exhausted or resumes >= max, or if a different live owner holds it, then derive attempt = locked quota.resumes + 1 and write running in the same locked write. Pre-lock checks stay only as fast-fail UX. Invariant: no spawn unless the locked record was resumable at the claim instant, and attempt numbers are monotone in locked state.
- **F03-5** [blocker] `crates/c3/src/work/record.rs:137`, `crates/c3/src/work/record.rs:187`, `crates/c3/src/work/record.rs:211` - Q3/F91-2 remedy: make admission fail closed on uninspectable records. read_record (record.rs:137) maps every fs::read error to Ok(None), run_dirs (record.rs:187) returns [] on read_dir failure and flattens entry errors, and live_run/other_live_run (record.rs:211/223) discard read errors via .ok(), so a live worker whose run.json is unreadable is invisible to the one-live-worker guard. Trigger: A worker is alive while a second process can take the admission lock but cannot read the live run.json, or hits an enumeration/entry error; the guard sees no live run and launches a second worker. Evidence: read-code: Err(_) => return Ok(None) at lines 139-142 swallows PermissionDenied identically to NotFound.; read-code: read_dir failure returns empty vec, entries iterate with flatten(), and live_run/other_live_run chain .ok().flatten() so parse/read errors read as absence. Verify: New tests read_record_distinguishes_notfound_from_unreadable (missing dir yields Ok(None); a run.json made unreadable, e.g. replaced by a directory, yields Err) and admission_refuses_when_candidate_uninspectable (other_live_run/live_run return Err and claim_for_spawn refuses before writing running). Plus the RC1 denied-read fixture with a live blocking worker: second same-task command refuses with no launch marker. Remedy: Distinguish error kinds: only ErrorKind::NotFound maps to Ok(None); all other read errors, plus directory enumeration and entry errors, propagate as Err. Change run_dirs, live_run and other_live_run to return Result and make both admission callers (run() start path and claim_for_spawn) refuse admission on Err. Invariant: admission proceeds only when every candidate record was inspected or confirmed absent; uninspectable state is a refusal, never an empty list. Parse errors already return Err and keep doing so.
- **F03-6** [major] `crates/c3/src/work/record.rs:544`, `crates/c3/src/work/engines.rs:481`, `crates/c3/src/work/engines.rs:503` - Q3/F91-4 remedy: require affirmative terminal evidence per engine. summarize_events (record.rs:544) sets is_error=false unless is_error is exactly true, so a bare {"type":"result"} reads as Claude success; terminal_outcome (engines.rs:504) sets saw_completed on a Codex agent_message item, and the existing unit test at engines.rs:651 enshrines agent_message-only as Success. Trigger: Exit-zero launcher emits only {"type":"result"} (Claude) or only an item.completed agent_message (Codex) and ends; the run is recorded done with no successful terminal event. Evidence: read-code: Any result line sets has_result=true; is_error becomes true only for boolean true, absent included. Claude maps has_result && !is_error to Terminal::Success.; read-code: item.completed with an agent_message item sets saw_completed exactly like turn.completed; test terminal_outcome_reads_the_stream asserts Success for an agent_message-only stream. Verify: Update terminal_outcome_reads_the_stream: agent_message-only Codex stream and bare-result Claude stream now yield Terminal::None (hence died via classify_attempt), while subtype success + is_error:false and turn.completed still yield Success. Add classify-level cases mirroring RC3 of handoff 91 for both engines at exit zero. Remedy: Claude: add has_success_result to EventSummary, set only when subtype == "success" and is_error is explicitly Some(false); terminal_outcome returns Success only from that field, Error on an explicit error result, else None. Codex: delete the agent_message arm so only turn.completed sets saw_completed; agent_message keeps feeding report extraction in summarize, which is a separate path. classify_attempt already fails closed on Terminal::None, so no caller change is needed. Invariant: done requires the engine's own successful terminal event, never the absence of an error marker.
- **F03-7** [note] (no location) - Q4: credit must be split, delayed, and denominated. Finder earns finder credit when a finding is verified real at closure; solver earns solver credit only when an adopted proposal is implemented and verified without material redesign (partly when the coordinator had to rework it). Adoption alone earns nothing scoreable, so raising easy blockers cannot farm solver or finder credit. Trigger: Router learns from adoption/verification marks; a finder could raise trivial blockers, or a solver could win adoptions with confident but unverifiable proposals. Evidence: inferred: Brief proposes adoption marks feed the router as a second score dimension; adoption is coordinator taste, verification is ground truth, and the two passes show findings vary widely in severity and recurrence. Verify: Backfill-score the two acceptance passes: check that credit lands only on the verified F89 closures, that the reopened F89-2/F89-3/F89-8 yield recurrence-weighted rather than fresh credit, and that no score moves on adoption alone. Remedy: Keep three separate router dimensions (finder, solver, verifier), each updated only at verified closure, weighted by severity and by whether the finding is a recurrence of a prior fix. The denominator counts rejected and duplicate findings against the finder, and rejected proposals against the solver, so volume without verification dilutes rather than accumulates. Require a minimum sample before solver routing outweighs the default roster.

