## Framing — challenge first

The premise 'the finder would confirm its own frame' is only half supported: pass 2's findings are *missed error paths and races*, not design disputes — the finder's one-line remedies were directions and the worker's designs were reasonable but incomplete. Enumeration, not taste, is failing. Cheaper levers the brief does not list: (a) require the worker's fix wave to include a self-adversarial pass ('enumerate every error return and race window in the functions you touched') before review; (b) one router-chosen solver instead of a panel of 3; (c) batch all blockers of a pass into ONE remedy consultation (~one 280 s pass, not 3×); (d) test-driven remedy — the solver ships the failing test first, the coordinator accepts the test, then judges patches only against it. To choose between these I would need the pass-to-closure history over more than the two passes observed, and the cost of one wrongly adopted design.

## Q1 — roles and verifier

Sound, with the verifier split in two: (1) a test the solver must ship — failing before, green after — which the coordinator RUNS (mechanical, no model); (2) the finder re-checks only its own trigger replay, binary closed/not-closed, and never sees rival designs, which removes the 'finder rejects every foreign fix' mode. A third reviewer adds cost and no authority. Solver-not-knowing-the-codebase is a pack problem: carry the named functions, their callers, and existing tests, not the tree. Cost is handled by scoping to blockers and batching.

## Q2 — minimal record and non-automation

`findings[].proposals[] {consult_id, member, approach, locations[], patch_sketch, test, risks, confidence, status: proposed|adopted|rejected, reason}` in `findings.json` — one file, matching the ledger; brief generated from the finding record + that pack; all members seated as solvers with the finder's vendor excluded. NOT automated: whether a blocker is real at all, which proposal is adopted, rejection wording, and any worker launch — all stay with the coordinator.

## Q3 — remedies (I am not the finder; details in findings #3–#6)

- **F91-1:** move status/exhaustion/owner validation inside `claim_for_spawn`, compute `n` from the locked reread, and reject on attempt-generation mismatch.
- **F91-2:** make `read_record`/`run_dirs`/`live_run`/`other_live_run` error-propagating; only confirmed NotFound is absence; refusal otherwise.
- **F91-3:** propagate event-file read errors; keep per-attempt files until their bytes are durably appended and classified; never overwrite a report from an unreadable source.
- **F91-4:** Claude success needs an explicit `is_error:false` result; Codex success needs `turn.completed` — agent_message feeds extraction only. Both fail closed to `died`.

## Q4 — scoring

Two router dimensions: `finder` and `solver`. A finder's finding matures only when a later pass closes it without regression — never-closing blockers stop scoring, which prices out easy blockers. Solver: adopted +1, verified (test green AND finder confirms trigger closed) +2, adopted-but-reblocked −1, rejected 0. Only outcomes move scores; the finder's taste over proposals does not.

## Requested checks

- **RC1:** repo root; workspace-write; `cargo test -p c3`; observe the suite is green so each proposal starts from a passing base; budget ~5 min.
- **RC2:** repo root; workspace-write; build the F91-2 fixture (deny-read ACL on a live run's run.json), start a second `c3 work`; observe refusal naming the unread record before any launch marker; budget 10 min.

