**Q1.** Separate responsibilities, but do not require three different models. The evidence shows incomplete state and failure-path coverage; it does not establish that finder-designed fixes cause the acceptance loop. Missing options include an executable reproducer before implementation, one solver plus an adversarial test reviewer, and a shared state-transition specification covering several findings.

Start with one solver per related invariant cluster. Allow the finder to propose a remedy; obtain independently drafted alternatives when warranted. Solver-authored tests are necessary evidence, not an independent verdict. The finder checks the original trigger; the coordinator checks implementation and regressions. Use a third reviewer for disputed criteria or substantial residual risk. Require a counterexample or violated criterion for rejection, preventing taste-based vetoes.

To choose panel size, measure recurrence causes, repository familiarity, pack completeness, available reviewer windows, and total time to verified closure. Vendor diversity is a useful preference, not proof of independence.

**Q2.** Pilot a `solver` role through existing consultations; introduce purpose `remedy` once outcomes justify separate routing. Minimum proposal: `proposal_id`, `finding_ids`, `consult_id`, reviewer identity, base revision/tree digest, finding snapshot digest, pack digest, approach, affected functions, patch sketch, invariant, executable test specification, assumptions and risks. Numerical confidence is optional.

Keep original replies immutable. Store proposal references/content under findings and append decision events containing actor, time, selected proposal IDs, rationale and implementation revision. A merged design needs its own identity and parent links. Alternatives are `not_selected`, not necessarily incorrect. Proposal adoption must not change finding status; verification requires recorded execution evidence.

Packs need callers, state writers, locking/liveness helpers, error paths, relevant tests and truncation disclosures—not only cited lines. Prefer another lab when qualified and available; preserve explicit coordinator-provider exclusions. Never silently relax exclusions, select/merge proposals, launch workers, mark verification, or spend consultation windows. Automatically preparing a costed command and validating references is reasonable.

**Q3. F91-1.** Change `claim_for_spawn`, `resume_once`, `do_resume_foreground` and `run_alarm`: pass an expected attempt/state snapshot and explicit foreground/alarm intent. Under `admission_lock`, reread, validate state, exhaustion, effective cap and owner identity, reject stale snapshots, then reserve the next attempt. Only the matching alarm owner may resume `waiting_quota`. Make recovery writes lock-protected; `read_record` currently mutates records. Invariant: every launch consumes one fresh, eligible claim. Test `resume_claim_rejects_stale_snapshot`: pause a second claimant, finish the first as done, exhausted quota, or another resumable generation; require rejection and no second launch. Include a successful alarm control.

**Q3. F91-2.** Make `read_record`, `run_dirs`, `live_run` and `other_live_run` propagate inspection failures; update `allocate` and admission callers. Replace `is_file()` filtering with fallible metadata inspection. Only confirmed absence is absence. Invariant: admission requires inspectable competing records. Test `admission_fails_closed_on_inspection_error`: inject read, metadata, enumeration, entry and parse failures while a worker remains live; require refusal before allocation/launch, plus absent-root and terminal-record controls.

**Q4.** Track adoption as preference, not correctness. Credit solver outcomes only after a linked implementation passes independently specified criteria; preserve partial attribution for merged proposals and worker changes. Credit finders for independently substantiated, deduplicated defects, never blocker counts or downstream adoption. Keep verification quality separate. Unselected proposals are unobserved outcomes. Use sample counts, shrinkage and exploration before routing strongly; compare closure time and reopen rate as well as correctness.

