## Framing first

The proposal answers a diagnosis the record does not support. Pass 1 raised 8; a worker fixed all 8; pass 2 found 4, two of them "the remaining case" of pass-1 blockers. That pattern is not *the finder's frame was confirmed by its own remedy*. It is *the remedy was a direction and the invariant was never written down*. Three labs proposing three designs fixes the **choice** problem; it does not produce the artifact that closes the loop — a test that fails before the fix and passes after. Without that, pass 3 will again say "the fix closed the simple case".

So: yes to solvers proposing remedies, but as the **second** intervention. Cheaper first moves the brief does not list:

- **Blank-slate re-design by the finder, without its own remedy text.** Frame-confirmation comes mostly from re-reading its own sentence; deleting it costs nothing.
- **Second-lab review of the diff, not design before it.** Reviewing 50 changed lines beats designing from a pack, and targets the missed case directly.
- **A deterministic schedule/state harness** for the admission family (F91-1/2/3 are all read-state-then-act races). One fixture harness kills the family; no amount of prose design does.
- **A no-panel fast path** where the design space is singular (F91-3 is an ordering fix).
- **An explicit "no proposal adopted" outcome.** Without it a panel always yields a fix and launders the decision.

Missing from the sketch: the **cost model**. Window %, not wall-clock, is scarce — N parallel solvers cost N × 280 s of window for one pass's wall-clock, and blockers arrive in families. Budget per family, cap at 2 solvers, pre-commit the window. Also, **vendor diversity is not independence**: shared training data and a shared brief give correlated proposals, and `--exclude-vendor` can leave one eligible solver. Require ≥2, else skip and record why. What I would need to choose: a replay of the F91-1 brief to two labs, diffed, to see whether proposals actually diverge (RC5).

## Q1

Separation is sound; the roles are mis-assigned. **Verifier ≠ finder.** The finder's acceptance criteria are already on record (`trigger`, `verification`), so make it a *challenger* with the mandate to find what the test misses, not to bless the fix. The verifier of record should be **the solver's test, rejected if it passes before the fix** — a third member only for judgement calls (severity, design), never for mechanical invariants. Failure modes: a pack-fed solver proposes plausible-but-wrong fixes the coordinator cannot rank without reading the code itself; the standing finder holds an effective veto over foreign fixes, so "the coordinator picks" collapses to "the finder approves" unless the finder's criteria are frozen *before* proposals land; and per-blocker panels multiply cost across a family. See finding #9.

## Q2

Minimal record: `findings[].proposals[] = {id, consult_id, proposer{provider,model,role}, approach, locations[], patch_sketch, test{name, drives, fails_before}, pack_digest, risks, confidence, decision{value, reason, decided_by, at}}`. Two additions matter: `test.fails_before` (what makes a proposal falsifiable) and `pack_digest` (what the solver actually saw — the sketch's real hole). `c3 findings set <id> --adopt <proposal-id> --reason` is the only writer of `decision`, refusing without `fails_before`. Must not be automated: choosing, marking verified, spending the window, merging (record `merged_from` or nothing), and the finder adding criteria after seeing proposals. See finding #10.

## Q3

Remedies for F91-1, F91-2, F91-3, F91-4 are findings #5–#8 (design, functions, invariant, test name each).

## Q4

Score signed, not counted. Solver +1 when the finding reaches `verified`; **reversed** if a later finding with the same trigger supersedes it — that is exactly what stops the router learning "good solver" from a fix that closed the simple case. Finder +1 for a finding that survives to `verified`, weighted by severity, and it may not rate the solver (the `useful` rating for a remedy consult is the coordinator's). Key solver score by (purpose, topic): an admission-lock fix is not evidence about a UI fix. See finding #11.

## Requested checks

- **RC1.** Repo root, read-only: `rg -n "fn .*test|#\[test\]" crates/c3/src/work/ crates/c3/tests/` — enumerates fixtures; settles which of F91-1..4 already have a deterministic test. Budget 2 min.
- **RC2.** Repo root, read-only: `rg -n "read_record\(|\.ok\(\)\.flatten\(\)|unwrap_or_default\(\)" crates/c3/src/work/` — counts call sites; sizes the strict/lenient split in finding #6. Budget 2 min.
- **RC3.** Repo root, read-only: `rg -n "proposals|remedy|prior_findings" crates/c3/src/consult/ crates/c3/src/findings*` — settles whether Q2 needs a schema change or only a store/CLI change. Budget 2 min.
- **RC4.** Repo root, read-only: `rg -n "role|brief" crates/c3/src/panel/roles.rs` — settles whether "you are a solver for F91-1" is expressible today or needs a per-seat brief. Budget 2 min.
- **RC5.** Repo root, workspace-write: replay the F91-1 brief to two labs via `c3 consult`, diff the `approach` fields — settles whether vendor diversity yields divergent designs before paying for the feature. Budget 10 min.


### Findings

- **F14-1** [major] `.collab/parity-0.6.1-2026-10-08/handoffs/91-codex-codex-0.2.3-acceptance-2.reply.json:6` - The remedy-panel proposal rests on a diagnosis the acceptance record contradicts. The pass-1 to pass-2 pattern (8 findings fixed, 4 returned, two of them 'the remaining case' of the same blockers) is evidence of under-specified invariants and absent regression tests, not of the finder confirming its own frame. Adding design alternatives does not produce the artifact that closes the loop: a test that fails before the fix and passes after. Trigger: Any blocker whose remedy is a one-sentence direction, implemented by a worker that makes the design choices alone. Evidence: read-code: Both carry supersedes: [F89-2] and [F89-8] with prose like 'the lock closes the straightforward simultaneous-live resume race, but the admission claim remains stale-state susceptible' - the previous remedy was followed, the invariant was not stated.; read-code: The invariant the fix must establish exists today only as a prose doc comment; nothing in the code or tests asserts it. Verify: Classify the 12 findings of passes 1-2 by cause (invariant unstated / test absent / design contested) and check whether the two 'remaining case' findings would have been caught by a test derived from the pass-1 trigger. If most are invariant-unstated, the panel is not the first intervention. Remedy: Make the finding's own artifact the acceptance criterion before any design work: require findings[].test {name, drives, fails_before} alongside remedy, where fails_before names the observed pre-fix result. Treat the test, not the finder's opinion, as the verifier of record. Build the remedy panel as the second intervention, scoped to findings whose cause is a contested design (e.g. the error-propagation API shape in F91-2), not to every blocker.
- **F14-2** [major] `.collab/consilium-2026-10-22/handoffs/01-claude-remedy-proposals.md:27` - The brief lists only one option (a cross-lab solver panel) for a problem with at least four cheaper alternatives, and no option for the case where no proposal is adoptable. Trigger: Any HOLD with blockers, where the coordinator must decide how to spend the window. Evidence: read-code: The proposal and its rationale are stated; no alternative intervention and no 'no proposal adopted' outcome appear anywhere in the brief.; inferred: F91-3 is an ordering defect (read source, then delete it; append before refresh) with a singular correct design - a design panel has nothing to choose between. Verify: For each of F91-1..F91-4, write down the number of genuinely distinct designs; if any is 1, that finding must not be sent to a panel. Remedy: Add these options to the decision list: (a) blank-slate re-design by the finder with its own remedy text withheld (removes frame-confirmation at zero vendor cost); (b) a second lab reviewing the implemented diff rather than designing from a pack (cheaper input, targets the missed case); (c) a one-time deterministic schedule/state harness for the read-state-then-act family F91-1/2/3; (d) a no-panel fast path for singular-design fixes; (e) an explicit 'no proposal adopted - the finder's remedy stands / escalate to the operator' outcome, without which a panel always yields a fix and launders the decision.
- **F14-3** [major] `.collab/consilium-2026-10-22/handoffs/01-claude-remedy-proposals.md:25` - The cost model in the brief is the wrong axis. Window percentage, not wall-clock seconds, is the scarce resource: N parallel solvers consume N x 280 s of window for one pass's wall-clock, so the panel does not save the 40-60 minute worker wave and multiplies the constraint that actually binds. Trigger: A HOLD with several blockers; the coordinator offers one --remedy line per blocker. Evidence: read-code: The brief records 280 s and 9-16% of a 5-hour window per pass and a 40-60 minute worker wave between passes, then proposes three consultations per blocker.; read-code: Pass 2 raised 4 findings at once; F91-1 and F91-2 are one admission-lock problem reported as two, so per-blocker panels would brief the same code twice. Verify: Sum the window percentage of the last three acceptance passes and compare it with the proposed three-consultation panel on the same findings. Remedy: Budget the panel in window percent and pre-commit it before the first solver call. Brief per finding family (F91-1+F91-2 are one admission brief), cap at two solvers, and require the coordinator to state the window it is willing to spend before the panel starts. Parallelism reduces wall-clock only, so never justify the panel by the worker wave.
- **F14-4** [major] `.collab/consilium-2026-10-22/handoffs/01-claude-remedy-proposals.md:51` - --exclude-vendor <finder's lab> can leave one or zero eligible solvers, and vendor diversity is not solver independence: models sharing training data and a shared brief produce correlated proposals, so 'one reviewer's taste becomes a decision between alternatives' is an assumption, not a property. Trigger: A roster with few labs, or a panel where every eligible member proposes the same approach. Evidence: assumed: Not checked: how many distinct labs the roster holds, so the number of solvers surviving an exclude-vendor filter is unknown.; read-code: Panels seat by willingness via Kuhn matching on score rank; nothing in the described mechanism guarantees a minimum number of eligible solvers after exclusion. Verify: Run RC5: send the F91-1 brief to two labs and diff the approach fields. Identical approaches settle the correlation question against the panel's rationale. Remedy: Require at least two eligible solvers after exclusion; otherwise skip the panel and record the skip and its reason in sessions.json rather than running a one-member panel that produces a single taste with panel provenance. Measure divergence on a replay before building the feature, and if proposals correlate, prefer option (b) - an independent diff review - which does not depend on the diversity assumption.
- **F14-5** [blocker] `crates/c3/src/work/mod.rs:747`, `crates/c3/src/work/mod.rs:1142`, `crates/c3/src/work/mod.rs:1386` - F91-1 remedy: make the admission lock the only place the spawn decision is made. claim_for_spawn takes a ClaimExpectation {allowed_statuses, expected_attempt, require_not_exhausted}; resume_once stops computing n before the lock and passes the generation it read; claim_for_spawn re-reads under the lock, computes n = quota.resumes + 1 from that same read, and refuses on terminal status, exhausted quota, a live foreign owner, or a generation mismatch. The pre-lock checks stay only as fast-fail UX and are explicitly non-authoritative. Trigger: Two resumes pass the quota/non-exhausted checks; the second pauses before claim_for_spawn while the first reaches done or exhausts the cap. Evidence: read-code: n is computed from a pre-lock read_record, then claim_for_spawn is called with that n as a parameter - the lock never sees the state n was derived from.; read-code: Under the lock the code checks other_live_run and a foreign live owner, then unconditionally sets status=running, pid, attempt=attempt and writes; terminal status, exhaustion and the attempt generation are not re-checked. Verify: Test crates/c3/tests/work_admission.rs::stale_resume_claim_is_refused - drive it with a test-only barrier hook injected between the pre-lock read and the lock acquisition: let the first resume reach done (case A) and then quota with exhausted=true (case B); require Err and no status write in both. Remedy: Change the signature to claim_for_spawn(work_root, rec_dir, expect: ClaimExpectation) -> Result<RunRecord, String>, compute the attempt number inside it from the locked read, and expose a #[cfg(test)] before_claim hook so the interleaving is deterministic. Invariant: every transition into running is decided from record state read under the admission lock, and the attempt number is a function of that same read.
- **F14-6** [blocker] `crates/c3/src/work/record.rs:137`, `crates/c3/src/work/record.rs:187`, `crates/c3/src/work/record.rs:211`, `crates/c3/src/work/record.rs:221` - F91-2 remedy: distinguish confirmed absence from failed inspection, and fail closed. Add read_record_strict returning Err for any error other than ErrorKind::NotFound and for parse failures; run_dirs returns Result and propagates read_dir and entry errors instead of flattening them; other_live_run and live_run return Result and propagate; claim_for_spawn refuses admission with the failing record named when any candidate cannot be inspected. Keep the lenient read_record for display paths (--status, --wait) so the blast radius stays inside admission. Trigger: A live worker holds a record that a second process can allocate a directory beside but cannot read (PermissionDenied, sharing violation), or an enumeration/entry error occurs. Evidence: read-code: std::fs::read error is mapped to Ok(None) for every ErrorKind, including access failures - only parse errors produce Err.; read-code: run_dirs returns an empty Vec on read_dir failure and uses rd.flatten() (entry errors dropped); live_run and other_live_run call read_record(&dir).ok().flatten(), so even the parse error read_record does produce is discarded before the liveness decision.; read-code: claim_for_spawn relies on other_live_run and a single read_record; a swallowed error at either point makes it proceed to write running. Verify: Test crates/c3/tests/work_admission.rs::unreadable_record_refuses_admission - create a live run directory, deny reads on its run.json (Windows share_mode(0) handle from a test helper; chmod 000 on Unix) and separately write unparseable bytes; require a refusal naming the unreadable record and no new launch marker in both cases. Remedy: Introduce the strict/lenient split rather than changing read_record in place, because read_record has many display callers (see RC2). Invariant: absence of a run is asserted only on confirmed NotFound; any unreadable or unparseable candidate record makes admission refuse. A directory that exists but cannot be inspected is never evidence that no run is live.
- **F14-7** [major] `crates/c3/src/work/mod.rs:858`, `crates/c3/src/work/mod.rs:1175`, `crates/c3/src/work/mod.rs:1192`, `crates/c3/src/work/mod.rs:868` - F91-3 remedy: make a read failure precede and prevent the destructive step. In resume_once, match on the read of tmp_events and tmp_stderr; on Err, call finish_artifact_error and return before append_resume and before the remove_file calls, so the per-attempt bytes survive. In refresh_artifacts, propagate the events.jsonl read error (and the read_record error at line 868) as Err so the caller records artifact_error without writing report.md; write report.md only after its source read has succeeded. Trigger: A resumed child writes a valid successful stream, then reading its temporary events file fails while removal would still succeed, or reading events.jsonl in refresh_artifacts fails transiently. Evidence: read-code: attempt_events uses unwrap_or_default, so a failed read yields an empty string; append_resume then succeeds appending nothing, and lines 1192-1193 remove both temporary files - the bytes are gone.; read-code: events.jsonl is read with unwrap_or_default and the resulting (empty) report is written unconditionally; only the writes are error-checked, not the read. Line 868 additionally defaults a failed read_record to RunRecord::default(), silently dropping prior summary fields.; read-code: On a successful refresh the record takes the status classified earlier from the attempt stream, including done, so an empty report can be recorded done. Verify: Test crates/c3/tests/work_artifacts.rs::artifact_read_failure_preserves_bytes - inject a read fault separately at the attempt-events site and the aggregate-events site; require status artifact_error, nonzero exit, .events-resume-N.jsonl still present with its original bytes, and report.md byte-identical to its pre-run content. Remedy: Reorder so no source is deleted or replaced before its read succeeded: read, verify, append durably, only then remove. Invariant: an artifact is replaced only from a source that was successfully read, and a failed read is artifact_error with a nonzero exit and retained source bytes. This is a singular design - do not send it to a design panel.
- **F14-8** [major] `crates/c3/src/work/engines.rs:504`, `crates/c3/src/work/engines.rs:480`, `crates/c3/src/work/record.rs:544`, `crates/c3/src/work/engines.rs:642` - F91-4 remedy: require affirmative, engine-specific success evidence and separate it from assistant-text extraction. Claude: treat a result as success only when is_error is present and false (or the result carries an explicit success subtype); a result missing the field is Terminal::None, not Success. Codex: delete the item.completed + agent_message arm from the success detector so only turn.completed sets saw_completed; agent_message remains in the report extractor. Trigger: An exit-zero launcher emits only {"type":"result"} for Claude, or only an item.completed agent_message for Codex, then ends without a successful turn terminal. Evidence: read-code: Claude returns Success whenever has_result is set and is_error is not set; Codex sets saw_completed for an item.completed agent_message as well as turn.completed, then returns Success on saw_completed alone.; read-code: The existing unit test encodes the defect as expected behaviour for the agent_message-only stream, so the test must be inverted, not merely extended.; read-code: classify_attempt maps Terminal::Success plus exit 0 to done, so an incomplete stream is recorded as a completed worker run. Verify: Extend crates/c3/src/work/engines.rs::terminal_outcome_reads_the_stream with the two stated streams: require Terminal::None for each, and require Success for a Codex stream containing turn.completed and for a Claude result with is_error present and false. Then require died, not done, through classify_attempt at exit 0. Remedy: Invariant: success requires an affirmative engine-specific success terminal; assistant text is never completion evidence. Keep the two concerns in separate functions so the report extractor can keep reading agent_message without conferring completion. This is a small, mechanical fix - pair it with the regression test rather than a solver panel.
- **F14-9** [major] `.collab/consilium-2026-10-22/handoffs/01-claude-remedy-proposals.md:64` - Q1 recommendation: the finder must not verify. It should be a challenger whose acceptance criteria are frozen on record before proposals land, and the verifier of record should be the solver's test, rejected if it passes before the fix; a third member verifies only judgement calls (severity, design), never mechanical invariants. Trigger: A blocker whose fix lands; the panel decides who signs it off. Evidence: read-code: Every verification field is a prospective instruction to run a fixture the finder designed (RC1-RC3 in its own reply); the finder is already specifying the proof, so its later verdict tests only that the proof was run, not that the fix generalises.; inferred: A standing finder that both designs the acceptance fixture and signs the fix has an effective veto over foreign designs; 'the coordinator picks' then collapses to 'the finder approves'. Verify: On the next blocker, record the finder's acceptance criteria before the proposals are read; if the finder adds a criterion after seeing a proposal, the process is not controlling the frame. Remedy: Freeze findings[].verification at raise time and forbid amendments once proposals exist. The solver ships the test; the coordinator requires it to fail against the pre-fix tree (fails_before recorded). The finder's next pass is a search for the uncovered case, not a sign-off. Reserve third-member verification for contested severity or design, where no test can adjudicate.
- **F14-10** [major] `.collab/consilium-2026-10-22/handoffs/01-claude-remedy-proposals.md:51`, `.collab/consilium-2026-10-22/handoffs/01-claude-remedy-proposals.md:68` - Q2 recommendation: proposals live in the finding, not a new store: findings[].proposals[] = {id, consult_id, proposer{provider,model,role}, approach, locations[], patch_sketch, test{name, drives, fails_before}, pack_digest, risks, confidence, decision{value, reason, decided_by, at}}. c3 findings set <id> --adopt <proposal-id> --reason is the single writer of decision and refuses without test.fails_before. pack_digest records what the solver actually saw. Trigger: A remedy consultation is run for one or more finding ids and the coordinator must record a choice. Evidence: read-code: The brief states the reply schema already carries findings[].remedy and prior_findings[].status with the proposed|implemented|verified|rejected lifecycle, and that c3 findings lists and sets status - so proposals extend an existing per-finding record rather than needing a new ledger.; assumed: Not read in this consultation: whether the reply schema already has a proposals field, so RC3 is needed to decide between a schema change and a store/CLI change only. Verify: Run RC3 and RC4: they settle whether proposals need a schema addition and whether a per-seat brief ('you are a solver for F91-1') is expressible with the existing --role/--roles plumbing. Remedy: Record exclusion of the finder's vendor and the panel's composition in sessions.json so a one-member or degenerate panel is visible in the record. Must NOT be automated: choosing the proposal, marking the finding verified, running the panel on the coordinator's behalf (the window spend is the coordinator's decision), merging two proposals without recording merged_from, and the finder amending acceptance criteria after proposals exist. Pack contents: the located functions with their enclosing context, the tests in the same module, the finding record verbatim, and the invariant documentation - sanitised by the existing pack rules, hashed into pack_digest.
- **F14-11** [major] `.collab/consilium-2026-10-22/handoffs/01-claude-remedy-proposals.md:77` - Q4 recommendation: score a signed ledger, not counters, and credit only verified outcomes with reversal on re-open. Solver +1 when the finding reaches verified; -1 if a later finding with the same trigger supersedes it. Finder +1 per finding that survives to verified, weighted by severity, and it may not rate the solver. Key solver score by (purpose, topic), not globally. Trigger: A proposal is adopted, the fix lands, and the next pass either closes or re-opens the finding. Evidence: read-code: Three pass-1 findings are marked still-open with a note that the pass-2 finding replaces the remaining claim - exactly the re-open event the scoring must reverse credit on.; assumed: Not read: whether ratings are stored as counters or as an append-only event log keyed by consult_id, which determines whether reversal is expressible without a migration. Verify: Check the router's rating store for a per-consult_id event log; if it is a counter, reversal needs a signed entry, which is the design decision this finding asserts. Remedy: Credit on verified, reverse on re-open, and require the reversal to reference the superseding finding id so the record shows why. Bounding easy-blocker gaming: weight by severity and require the coordinator to flag a finding's trigger as reachable, so a blocker with a trivial trigger earns little. Keep finder and solver scores in separate dimensions so a good solver with a mediocre finder record is routable as a solver.

